{"reference":"1 Corinthians 10","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":10,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Paul retells Israel’s wilderness experiences as a warning against confidence that ignores the danger of idolatry and other forms of unfaithfulness. His reading is a later canonical reuse of these traditions; it should be distinguished from reading Exodus on its own terms, where the central focus is the LORD’s deliverance and covenant relationship with Israel.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:1-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_010_8d74a29d85f07ae6be50f039","reader_path_1_corinthians_010_4308ba6191414a57e90f3a60"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Meals, Participation, and Food","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter does not treat every encounter with food in the same way. Paul distinguishes participation at a cultic table, meat bought in the market, and a meal offered in a private home. This distinction explains why he can forbid one setting while permitting food in another: the issue is not that every piece of food is inherently contaminated, but that a meal can carry a particular social and religious meaning.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:14-22","1 Corinthians 10:23-33","1 Corinthians 10:27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_010_c9a34efc8e370d2019301a1a"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul reasons from shared meals. Those who eat sacrifices participate in relation to the altar, and so the Lord’s cup and table cannot be combined with a demonic cup and table. At the same time, Paul denies that an idol or an idol-sacrifice has independent significance. The argument therefore preserves both parts of his reasoning: a warning about cultic participation without treating all food as demonic substance.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:18","1 Corinthians 10:19-20","1 Corinthians 10:20-22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_010_e13ebce53f7a2eab13d3e18f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"The cup and broken bread are described as participation in Messiah’s blood and body, and the one bread is connected with one body among many people. Later in the chapter, if someone identifies food as sacrificial, Paul directs abstention for the sake of the person who supplied that information and the conscience at stake. Shared eating is thus presented as both communal and socially meaningful.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:16-17","1 Corinthians 10:28-30"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_010_2c1294fe7063190407124a03"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Freedom and Conscience","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"“All things are lawful” is likely a claim that Paul quotes or qualifies rather than an unrestricted rule. He measures freedom by benefit, upbuilding, and another person’s good. In the market case, food may be eaten without investigation; in the hospitality case, disclosure of sacrificial provenance changes the social meaning of the meal. Here conscience concerns awareness and relationship, not an infallible private instinct or a tool for controlling someone else.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:18","1 Corinthians 10:23-24","1 Corinthians 10:25-26","1 Corinthians 10:25-30"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_010_d84fd877e1e7104c9827059d","reader_path_1_corinthians_010_4acfd43630443d35d23bf7f2"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why This Distinction Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The distinction among cultic participation, ordinary purchase, and private hospitality keeps Paul’s warning from becoming a blanket rule about food. It also makes freedom relational: the question is not only what a person may do, but whether an action builds up another person and respects the meaning a meal has acquired in that setting.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:14-15","1 Corinthians 10:18","1 Corinthians 10:23-24","1 Corinthians 10:25-26","1 Corinthians 10:28-30"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_010_b92551fdfce31de9a7466d22","reader_path_1_corinthians_010_b2b8217c772e5b881c3b3c73"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"The image of one bread and one body presses toward solidarity rather than exclusion based on class, race, disability, poverty, or social standing. Likewise, Paul’s warning about Israel addresses the assembly and cannot be used to blame Jewish people collectively.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:1-13","1 Corinthians 10:16-17","1 Corinthians 10:19-22","1 Corinthians 10:26"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_010_f154a01281ea17f63c1d41fd","reader_path_1_corinthians_010_9405334aeaee46ce9d7d8687"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Next Verse","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"The thought continues into 11:1, where imitation of Paul is qualified by his imitation of Messiah. This limits imitation language: it does not grant a leader unlimited control, while keeping Jews, Greeks, and God’s assembly visible in the chapter’s closing concern for others.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_010_da1922bda4a9d6c0c05469d0"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}